                "text": "I'm checking which shape is closest to the 8 at (5,4), comparing distances to the 3-shape, 6-shape, and 9-shape, and finding the 3 at (8,4) is nearest at distance 3.\n\nBut then looking at other training examples, nearest distance alone doesn't explain the chosen shape - in train3 the 8 selects the 5-shape even though the 9-shape is closer by Euclidean distance. So I'm testing a diagonal-alignment idea instead: checking whether the nearest cell of each shape sits at an exact diagonal offset from the 8, since that seems to match why 5 was picked over 9 and 4 in train3. I'm now applying this diagonal alignment check to train4's 8 at (5,3) against the 1-shape.\n\nRechecking train3 with Euclidean distance gives a tie between 9 and 4, but the actual answer was 5, so distance alone isn't the rule. It must be about direction: tracing a diagonal ray from the 8 cell upward-left hits the 5 cell first, before reaching any other digit, which explains the choice.\n\nI'm testing this ray-casting idea on the other examples. Train1 hits a 2 diagonally at distance 2 before hitting the 7 at distance 4 along another direction, matching the expected result. Train4 shows both the up and left rays reaching the same digit, 1, at equal distance, so that one's unambiguous. Train2 is trickier since a downward ray hits a 6 at distance 3 while a diagonal ray seems to hit a 0 rather than a 3, so I need to reexamine that case more carefully.\n\nFor the test grid, checking all four rays from the 8 at (5,4), the down-left diagonal reaches a 3 at distance 2, which is closer than any other digit hit, so the 3-shape wins and should be outlined orthogonally around the 8 using the cells forming that shape.\n\nContinuing to map out the grid, I'm tracking where each digit value (6, 8, 9, 3) appears across rows 2 through 9, building up the full coordinate picture row by row.\n\n",
